::ALG2_U01_L04_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is the simplified form of &radic;-64? {
= 8i #Correct. Since &radic;-64 = &radic;64 &middot; &radic;-1, the simplified form is 8i.
~ -8 #Squaring -8 gives positive 64, so this does not represent the square root of -64.
~ -8i #This has the wrong sign for the principal square root. The simplified principal square root is 8i.
~ 64i #This uses the radicand instead of its square root. The square root of 64 is 8.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is the discriminant of x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 10 = 0? {
~ 76 #This adds 4ac instead of subtracting it. The discriminant is b squared minus 4ac.
= -4 #Correct. b squared minus 4ac is 36 - 40, which equals -4.
~ 4 #This has the correct magnitude but the wrong sign. The subtraction gives a negative value.
~ -76 #This does not use the discriminant expression correctly.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>If the discriminant of a quadratic equation is negative, what type of solutions does the equation have? {
~ Two real solutions #A positive discriminant gives two real solutions.
~ One repeated real solution #A discriminant of zero gives one repeated real solution.
= Two complex solutions #Correct. A negative discriminant means the square root part of the quadratic formula is imaginary.
~ No solutions in the complex number system #Quadratic equations with real coefficients still have complex solutions when the discriminant is negative.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of x<sup>2</sup> + 9 = 0? {
~ x = 3 and x = -3 #Those values solve x squared minus 9 equals 0, not x squared plus 9 equals 0.
~ x = 9i and x = -9i #This uses 9 instead of the square root of 9.
= x = 3i and x = -3i #Correct. x squared equals -9, so x equals plus or minus 3i.
~ x = -3 only #There are two complex solutions, not one real solution.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 8 = 0? {
~ x = 2 and x = -2 #These are real values and do not satisfy the original equation.
= x = 2 + 2i and x = 2 - 2i #Correct. The quadratic formula gives (4 plus or minus &radic;-16) divided by 2, which simplifies to 2 plus or minus 2i.
~ x = -2 + 2i and x = -2 - 2i #The real part should be positive because -b divided by 2a is 4 divided by 2.
~ x = 4 + 2i and x = 4 - 2i #This does not divide the numerator by 2 completely.
}
